rent shapes  that were noted on the previous slides  the metadata there  for which room it was and then  if it was integrated into the chromosome  there it showed in the in the red  squares but again there's puva o1  across all these different clades of  citrobacter  carrying the kpc gene whereas before  we'd seen that the kpc gene  often had moved out of pova1 as a  background  but what we also see is that um  pvo1 or the the kpc gene is actually in  multiple plasmid backgrounds  in different clades as well  um the pac-man-like shapes are where we  couldn't complete or close the the  plasmid structure and so sort of just to  summarize that data  really this this plasmid puv801 sort of  shown there at the very top of the  square  had the most kpc across the most strains  and species  there was a little bit more sharing of  some ink ends  um across some strains and species but  for the for the most part  the plasmid that was shared amongst all  of these isolates from the environment  was pvo1 and interestingly  a lot of these isolates didn't share the  same plasmid  across different so we know that puvo1  is spreading or sorry kpc is spreading  at all levels via pvo1 by plasmid  by binary fission carrying the same  plasmid and by transposon  and even maybe a second transposon  moving all over the place  none of which we would have seen if we  just did short read sequencing  so to complete to finish you know it's  important to understand that  mobile genetic elements are moving genes  of drug resistance all around  i think this is probably happening all  the time in nature  and we just don't see it because we  don't have  that consequential of a gene  with a natural experiment where we were  tracking that specific gene over a long  period of time in the environment and in  patients  i think that long read sequencing if you  really want to understand the genetic  context for mobile genetic elements  long-range sequencing is critical  because as i've shown you can get r