 that 70 of our outbreak was hollow  circles  so the patient didn't have any ward  contact with any other patient that had  the same  strain and so definitely not a clonal  outbreak there was a strain circulating  in the united states  that had um of club pneumo  or is still circulating in the united  states but a lot of those patients  indicated here by imported meaning they  had it on admission  by exes here we did have some  overlap and potentially some plausible  transmission here of this anaerobacter  strain  but a lot of no ward contact  i'll also highlight that we had these  huge gaps in time between seeing a  strain so you'd see a strain here and  then it'd be two years later you'd see  the same strain in a different patient  as though we had another reservoir  so we knew that this was not the primary  mechanism to spread we kind of knew that  because  we had a multi-species outbreak  so we assumed that what was happening  was plasmid transmission so  for those you know who think about this  stuff a lot you know plasmids are the  little bits of dna much smaller than the  chromosome usually anywhere from  5 to 300  kilobases in size  it means that they have an ability to  replicate within the cell and so  we were thinking that if this is the kpc  gene we knew it was on a transposon  and we knew most of those transposons  were on plasmids  and it was moving between all these  different species so we thought we had a  plasmid-mediated outbreak  early in our journey we went ahead and  sequenced  through a combination of long and short  read  the index patients plasmids because we  figured okay so this patient has  brought it into the hospital it's  probably one of those clebnew or club  oxytocoplasmics that's been transmitted  down the line and so this  plasmid shown here which we dumped pkbc  uva01  was completely novel except for  this bit here that we'll get into but  the transposon  and then it was sandwiched in another  transposon but the rest  of the stock had never been seen in ncbi  at that time  and that was from our index patients  club pneumo  so we had a hunch um partly from just  some molecular  uh work that i'd done early on in some  southern plotting that puv801  was the plasmid transmitting kpc to all  these different species  and throughout our outbreak so  um when i paired up with uh  derek crooks group at oxford anna  shepard  um who is a very gifted bioinformaticist  um was the person that i collaborated  with on this project  and so she thought this was a very  interesting problem  and was very curious just to see if  whether or not we could  solve is it a plasma media outbreak by  looking at short read sequencing  and so again what we what we did was  generate you know plasmid specific  references  from the index patient and then look  um by mapping in to see if that plasmid  was present across the outbreak and with  using a definition of 99  sequence identity over 80 percent of the  reference length um  we kind of ran this across the entire  281 isolates  and here is a here are the phylogenetic  trees of all the different species  and shown here you can see sort of this  is our enterobacter for example a lot of  different  strains as you saw in the previous  picture  and anywhere there's a red dot pova1 was  in that isolate  and so you can see here we have a lot of  red dots  interestingly the blue dot was  the pain the index patient's other  plasmid  so that club oxytocoplasm is shown here  in club oxychocal but also we  we were able to map that plasmid into  some club pneumo into some citrobacter  so it really did look like from our  index  patient pova01 and pvo2 were widely  dispersed among species and strains  we figured it out it is a plasma  mediated outbreak  and again this was all from short read  sequencing but anna shepard  said you know hold on you can't say that  because  you don't know if the plasmid is  contiguous  with the transposon because there's  redundancy around mobile genetic  