 understand how much plasmid  how much transposon and how much strain  variability do you see if you sequence  every single isolate from that  longitudinal  period so  um we ended up doing about 120  isolates by a long read sequencing we  found  17 strains 12 species  and shown here in this is the different  rooms  as you can see here so at the top kind  of  bar there is room three one two eight  and then the bathroom  in sync in three one two eight and then  down below again  the room the bathroom sink and then uh  below that  is room b type uh three one eight two  and three one nine four  the way we've um done this is we took  all the isolates we had to short read  and long read sequences  we took the chromosome mapped it to a  reference and compared them  and so each strain or each clade  gets a different shape and so we color  coded them by species shown there  on the bottom and then the strains are  shown with different shapes  so what you can see is is there's a lot  of different  strains and species across these sinks  but what i was really trying to get at  was  what about the plasmids so just  interestingly  what's shown here and one of the  surprising things is there was a lot of  chromosomal  integration of kpc and so shown on the  left panel  is all the places where the kpc gene  and transposon had integrated into the  chromosome  there on the right hand side of the  slide  is all the plasmids  but what we found taking and just  going back to this slide you can see  there's the most kind of strain  diversity  among citrobacter so taking a deep dive  into the citrobacter across  all these different rooms um kind of  annotated their  room and sink location and whatnot  we took all the long read sequences of  the plasmids from these different  citrobacter and what you find  is if there is a square around it it  contains the kpc  gene so again kind of going across  here's the phylogenetic tree of the  plasmid  um shown on the left hand side with  those different shapes  that were noted on the previous slides  the metadata there  for which room it was and then  if it was integrated into the chromosome  there it showed in the in the red  squares but again there's puva o1  across all these different clades of  citrobacter  carrying the kpc gene whereas before  we'd seen that the kpc gene  often had moved out of pova1 as a  background  but what we also see is that um  pvo1 or the the kpc gene is actually in  multiple plasmid backgrounds  in different clades as well  um the pac-man-like shapes are where we  couldn't complete or close the the  plasmid structure and so sort of just to  summarize that data  really this this plasmid puv801 sort of  shown there at the very top of the  square  had the most kpc across the most strains  and species  there was a little bit more sharing of  some ink ends  um across some strains and species but  for the for the most part  the plasmid that was shared amongst all  of these isolates from the environment  was pvo1 and interestingly  a lot of these isolates didn't share the  same plasmid  across different so we know that puvo1  is spreading or sorry kpc is spreading  at all levels via pvo1 by plasmid  by binary fission carrying the same  plasmid and by transposon  and even maybe a second transposon  moving all over the place  none of which we would have seen if we  just did short read sequencing  so to complete to finish you know it's  important to understand that  mobile genetic elements are moving genes  of drug resistance all around  i think this is probably happening all  the time in nature  and we just don't see it because we  don't have  that consequential of a gene  with a natural experiment where we were  tracking that specific gene over a long  period of time in the environment and in  patients  i think that long read sequencing if you  really want to understand the genetic  context for mobile genetic elements  long-range sequencing is critical  because as i've shown you can get r